CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ constexpr derived-to-base pack call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.deduct.call]/4.3 + [dcl.constexpr] + [temp.point]/1 + [temp.inst]/5:
a constexpr function template with a derived-to-base pack parameter, called with
a derived-class argument.

Regression: a constexpr callee was converted eagerly/inline at the reference
(to permit constant folding) even for an ordinary run-time call, and in that
nested context the derived-to-base pack call was not built -- the callee body
and its sub-instantiations were dropped, so the call returned a nondet
reference (and the throw unwound the enclosing body, silently dropping main's
assertions: a vacuous "VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL").  Fixed by converting a
constexpr specialization eagerly only when instantiated from a
constant-expression context (folding required); reached for a run-time call it
is deferred to the clean method-body drain like any function, so the
derived-to-base pack call resolves.  Assertion 2 (a wrong value) FAILs, proving
non-vacuity.
